# Clawmacs TUI Chat Buffer -- Design Spec

Version: 0.1 -- 2026-03-14

## Overview

Clawmacs is a Common Lisp terminal UI application for interacting with
LLM agents. It is designed around Emacs metaphors (buffers, faces,
keymaps, commands) implemented with idiomatic Common Lisp (CLOS,
generic functions, conditions/restarts, special variables).

This spec covers the first iteration: a single-buffer chat TUI with a
stubbed agent backend.

## Goals

1. Render a chat buffer with scrollable history and a multi-line input
   area using croatoan (ncurses wrapper for CL).
2. Establish the core abstractions: messages, buffers, faces, commands,
   keymaps, and access control.
3. Stub the agent interaction so the TUI can be developed and tested
   independently of any LLM backend.

## Non-Goals (this iteration)

- Real LLM API integration.
- Multiple buffers or windows.
- Buffer-level point and mark (message selection).
- Face color transforms (lighten, darken, hue-rotate).
- Persistent sessions or serialization.
- Configuration file loading.

## System Structure

Single ASDF system `clawmacs`, single package `clawmacs`.

### Dependencies

- `croatoan` -- terminal UI
- `alexandria` -- standard CL utility library

### File Layout

```
clawmacs/
  clawmacs.asd
  src/
    packages.lisp       ; package declaration
    faces.lisp          ; face protocol and definitions
    message.lisp        ; message object (lines, point, mark)
    buffer.lisp         ; buffer object (doubly-linked message list)
    commands.lisp       ; defcommand macro, access control, keymaps
    render.lisp         ; croatoan rendering
    main.lisp           ; entry point, event loop
```

## Data Model

### Message

Each message is a rich object analogous to a Hemlock buffer. It
contains its own line structure, point, and mark for intra-message
navigation and text editing.

```
message
  - lines:       doubly-linked list of line objects
  - point:       (line . offset) -- cursor position within the message
  - mark:        (line . offset) or nil
  - sender:      keyword (:user or agent keyword, e.g., :claude-opus)
  - timestamp:   universal-time integer
  - face-set:    reference to the sender's face-set
  - read-only-p: boolean (t for history, nil for current input)
  - next:        pointer to next message in buffer
  - prev:        pointer to previous message in buffer
```

A line object holds a string. Lines within a message form a
doubly-linked list. Text properties (for inline face changes like
code spans or bold) are not needed this iteration; the line object
is intentionally minimal.

```
line
  - content: string
  - next:    line or nil
  - prev:    line or nil
```

### Buffer

The buffer owns a doubly-linked list of messages. The input area is
the mutable message at the tail of this list.

```
buffer
  - name:              string (e.g., "clawmacs:session-01")
  - first-message:     head of message linked list
  - last-message:      tail of message linked list (the input message)
  - input-message:     alias for last-message; invariant: always has read-only-p = nil.
                       This must be enforced: last-message and input-message always
                       refer to the same object.
  - agent-name:        string (name of the currently active agent)
  - working-directory: pathname
  - token-count:       integer
  - context-limit:     integer
  - status:            keyword (:idle, :thinking, :error)
  - face-registry:     hash-table mapping sender keywords to face-sets
  - keymap:            the active keymap
```

When the user sends a message:

1. The input message is finalized: `read-only-p` set to t, timestamped,
   sender set to `:user`.
2. A new empty input message is created and linked as the new tail.
3. `send-to-agent-with-context` is called with the buffer.
4. The stub creates a new agent message (read-only), inserts it before
   the input message, and returns.

## Face System

### Face Object

```
face
  - name:       keyword (e.g., :default, :bold, :code-block, :error)
  - foreground: color-spec or nil (inherit from parent)
  - background: color-spec or nil (inherit from parent)
  - bold-p:     boolean or nil (inherit from parent)
  - underline-p: boolean or nil (inherit from parent)
  - reverse-p:  boolean or nil (inherit from parent)
  - parent:     face or nil
  - transform:  transform-spec or nil (stub -- ignored in this iteration)
```

### Color Spec

```
color-spec
  - type:  keyword (:cga, :256, :hex)
  - value: integer (0-15 for CGA, 0-255 for 256-color)
           or string ("#rrggbb" for hex)
```

All three formats are supported. Terminal capability detection
determines which format is actually used for rendering. Hex colors are
downsampled to the best available terminal color.

### Face Set

```
face-set
  - owner: keyword (:user or agent keyword)
  - faces: hash-table mapping face-name keywords to face objects
```

### Inheritance Resolution

`resolve-face` is a pure function that walks up the parent chain to
fill in nil attributes. The chain terminates at a root face with all
attributes defined.

Transform specs are present in the data model but `resolve-face`
ignores them in this iteration, passing through the inherited value
unchanged.

### Default Face Sets

**User:**

| Face | Background | Foreground | Other |
|------|-----------|------------|-------|
| `:default` | CGA dark-gray (#8) | white | -- |

**Agent (first agent):**

| Face | Background | Foreground | Other |
|------|-----------|------------|-------|
| `:default` | black (#0) | white | -- |

**Shared (fallback):**

| Face | Background | Foreground | Other |
|------|-----------|------------|-------|
| `:modeline` | CGA white (#7) | black | bold |
| `:error` | inherit | CGA red (#4) | -- |
| `:system` | inherit | CGA cyan (#6) | -- |

## Command System & Access Control

### Command Metadata

```
command-metadata
  - name:        symbol
  - permission:  keyword (:user-only, :agent-with-permission, :agent-allowed)
  - docstring:   string
  - keybindings: list of key-specs
```

### Special Variables

```
*current-caller*  ; bound to :user or an agent keyword during command dispatch
*sandbox-root*    ; bound to a pathname -- restricts file operations
```

### defcommand Macro

`defcommand` expands to:

1. A generic function definition for the command.
2. An `:around` method that checks `*current-caller*` against the
   command's permission tag.
3. Registration of command metadata in a global command table.

Example:

```lisp
(defcommand send-message (:permission :user-only
                          :keys ((#\Return)))
  "Send the current input message to the agent."
  (buffer)
  ...)
```

### Access Control Enforcement

| Permission | Behavior |
|-----------|----------|
| `:agent-allowed` | `:around` method passes through |
| `:agent-with-permission` | `:around` signals `permission-required` condition; user gets `grant-permission` restart in the TUI; denied if not granted |
| `:user-only` | `:around` signals `permission-denied` (no restart); command is excluded from `list-available-commands` when `*current-caller*` is not `:user` |

The `list-available-commands` function filters by permission based on
`*current-caller*`. When an agent queries available tools, `:user-only`
commands do not appear -- enforcement by omission.

### Keymaps

```
keymap
  - name:     keyword
  - bindings: hash-table mapping key-event -> command-symbol
  - parent:   keymap or nil (fallback lookup)
```

Lookup walks the parent chain. The buffer holds the active keymap.

### Initial Keybindings

| Key | Command | Permission |
|-----|---------|------------|
| `RET` / `C-m` | `send-message` | `:user-only` |
| `C-j` | `insert-newline` | `:user-only` |
| printable chars | `self-insert` | `:user-only` |
| `C-a` | `move-beginning-of-line` | `:user-only` |
| `C-e` | `move-end-of-line` | `:user-only` |
| `C-k` | `kill-line` | `:user-only` |
| `C-y` | `yank` | `:user-only` |
| `C-x C-c` | `quit` | `:user-only` |

All commands are `:user-only` in this iteration since the agent is
stubbed.

## TUI Layout

```
+------------------------------------------------------------------+
|                                                                    |
|  agent> Hello, how can I help you today?                          |
|                                                                    |
|  user> Can you explain how the face system works?                 |
|                                                                    |
|  agent> The face system uses inheritance...                       |
|         Each agent has a default face that sets                   |
|         the background color for all their messages.              |
|                                                                    |
|                        (chat history region)                       |
|                                                                    |
+------------------------------------------------------------------+
|  > this is where I type                                           |
|    and it can be multiple lines                                   |
|                                                                    |
+------------------------------------------------------------------+
| clawmacs:session-01 | claude-opus | ~/proj   0/200k | idle       |
+------------------------------------------------------------------+
```

### Window Structure

Two croatoan windows stacked vertically:

1. **Buffer window** -- fills all rows except the bottom modeline row.
   Contains both the chat history (read-only messages) and the input
   message as a continuous stream. The input message is always visible
   at the bottom of this window. History scrolls above it. The cursor
   lives here, positioned within the input message.

2. **Modeline window** -- exactly 1 row, pinned to the last row of
   the terminal. Rendered by `render-modeline`.

### Modeline Format

```
 buffer-name | agent-name | working-directory     token-count/context-limit | status
 [---------- left-aligned --------------------]   [------ right-aligned ----]
```

Padded with spaces between left and right sections.

### Input Area Behavior

The input area is the unsent message at the tail of the buffer's
message list. It starts at minimum 3 rows and grows as the user types
(up to `(floor terminal-height 3)` rows maximum), reducing the visible
history area.

| Key | Action |
|-----|--------|
| `RET` / `C-m` | Send message (finalize, call `send-to-agent-with-context`) |
| `S-RET` / `C-j` | Insert newline |
| Printable characters | `self-insert` at point |

### Rendering

Rendering is driven by generic functions:

- `render-history (buffer window)` -- walks the message list, renders
  each with its sender prefix and face-set background color. Handles
  scrolling so the most recent messages and the input area are visible.
- `render-modeline (buffer window)` -- formats the modeline string
  with left/right alignment.
- `render-input (buffer window)` -- renders the input message with
  cursor. Kept separate from `render-history` because the input
  message requires cursor rendering and dynamic height calculation,
  even though both share the same croatoan window.

Each render function reads buffer state and writes to a croatoan
window. Mutation is isolated to croatoan's screen update.

### Event Loop

```
1. croatoan:event-case on the buffer window
2. On key event:
   a. Look up key in active keymap (walk parent chain)
   b. If found: bind *current-caller* to :user, call the command
   c. If not found and printable: call self-insert
3. After any state change: re-render both windows
4. On terminal resize: recalculate window dimensions, re-render
```

## Stub Agent

`send-to-agent-with-context` is a function that takes a buffer,
reads the last user message, creates a new agent message containing
the user's text prefixed with "Echo: ", and inserts it into the
buffer before the input message.

This stub allows full TUI development and testing without any LLM
dependency.

## Design Principles

Per `docs/DESIGN.md`:

- **Functional-first:** Pure functions for face resolution, modeline
  formatting, message text extraction, context assembly. Side effects
  isolated to rendering and event handling.
- **Immutability in functions:** Functions that compute (resolve-face,
  format-modeline, list-available-commands) are pure. Mutable state
  exists at the UI boundary (doubly-linked lists, buffer slots,
  croatoan windows).
- **Static typing discipline:** SBCL `declaim` and `declare` forms
  on all functions. Type declarations for slots in CLOS classes.
- **Emacs metaphors for UI:** Buffers, faces, keymaps, commands, point,
  mark, kill/yank.
- **Lispy idioms for code:** CLOS generic functions, special variables
  for dynamic context, conditions/restarts for access control,
  `defcommand` macro for the command DSL.

## Synthesis: Emacs Metaphors + Common Lisp

| Emacs Concept | CL Implementation | Advantage over Emacs |
|---------------|-------------------|---------------------|
| Buffers | CLOS objects | Typed slots, generic function dispatch |
| Buffer-local variables | CLOS slots | No special-case machinery needed |
| Modes | CLOS mixins | Method combination instead of ad-hoc mode maps |
| Faces | CLOS objects with parent chain | Explicit inheritance, color-space-aware |
| Commands | Generic functions via `defcommand` | Access control, dispatch on buffer type |
| Keymaps | Hash tables with parent chain | Immutable composition via `merge-keymaps` |
| Hooks | Lists of functions | Same as Emacs -- no tension |
| `condition-case` | CL conditions/restarts | Interactive recovery (retry, edit-and-resend) |
| Customization via setq + add-hook | Specialize generic functions | Users extend by defining methods |
